<%-
  _rlib   = context.respond_to?('r_libpath') ? context.r_libpath.to_s : ''
  _course = _rlib.sub(%r{/R/.*\z}, '')
  _slug   = context.respond_to?('course_slug') ? context.course_slug.to_s : ''
  _slug   = 'course' if _slug.empty?
  _tools  = "~/#{_slug}-job-tools"
  _img    = context.respond_to?('imagefile') ? context.imagefile.to_s : ''
  _image  = _img.empty? ? '' : "/shared/apptainerImages/#{_img}"
-%>
# Running an R script as a batch job

RStudio gives you one machine for as long as your session is open. A **batch
job** is different: you hand the cluster an R script, it finds a machine, runs
the script there, and writes the output to a file. You can close RStudio and
the job keeps going.

This folder — `<%= _tools %>/` — has everything you need. It is in
your own home directory, and it is put there fresh every time you open RStudio.

---

## Run your first job

Say your script is `hw3.R`, in whatever folder you are working in.

**1. Open the Terminal pane in RStudio.** It is the tab next to Console.

**2. Go to the folder your script is in.**

```bash
cd ~/my-folder
```

**3. Submit it.**

```bash
sbatch <%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh hw3.R
```

You will see:

```text
Submitted batch job 12345
```

That number is your **job ID**.

**4. Wait, then read the output.**

```bash
squeue -u $USER                 # still running? empty means it finished
cat <%= _slug %>-12345.out               # what your script printed
```

That is the whole thing. You do not have to copy the script, edit it, or set
anything up first.

---

## Where the output goes

Everything your script printed — `print()`, `cat()`, warnings, errors — goes
into one file, in the folder you submitted from:

```text
<%= _slug %>-<job id>.out
```

Files your script *writes itself* (a CSV, a plot, a saved model) go wherever
your script tells them to, exactly as they would in the console. Use a path
inside your own folder, not the course folder — the course folder is read-only
to you.

---

## Ask for more CPUs or more time

The defaults are **1 CPU** and **15 minutes**. If your job needs more, say so on
the command line:

```bash
sbatch -c 4 -t 02:00:00 run-r-job.sh hw3.R
```

| Option | Means | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `-c` | how many CPUs | `-c 4` |
| `-t` | how long, as `HH:MM:SS` | `-t 02:00:00` |
| `-J` | a name, so you can spot it in `squeue` | `-J hw3` |

**Options go before the script name.** This matters more than it looks:

```bash
sbatch -c 4 <%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh hw3.R    # correct
sbatch <%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh hw3.R -c 4    # WRONG - and silent
```

In the second one your job still runs, but on 1 CPU. The `-c 4` was handed to
your R script instead of to Slurm, and nothing warns you. If your job takes
just as long as before, check this first.

### If your job runs out of time

It is killed, and the output file stops mid-sentence. There is no other
warning. Ask for more time with `-t` and submit it again.

---

## Pass arguments to your R script

Anything after the script name is given to the script:

```bash
sbatch run-r-job.sh analysis.R data.csv 10000
```

Read them in R with:

```r
args <- commandArgs(trailingOnly = TRUE)
args[1]   # "data.csv"
args[2]   # "10000"   (a string - use as.numeric() if you need a number)
```

---

## Keep an eye on your jobs

```bash
squeue -u $USER          # what is queued or running right now
sacct -u $USER           # what has run today, and how it ended
scancel 12345            # stop job 12345
scancel -u $USER         # stop all of your jobs
```

In `squeue`, the `ST` column is the status: `R` is running, `PD` is waiting for
a free machine.

Your jobs are named `<%= _slug %>` unless you passed `-J`. Your RStudio session
appears here too, as a separate row — do not cancel that one by mistake.

---

## Make your own version

You will probably want your own copy at some point — different defaults, or an
extra step before R starts.

**Give it a new name when you copy it:**

```bash
cp <%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh run-my-job.sh
```

Then edit and submit `run-my-job.sh`. It is yours; nothing will ever overwrite
it.

> **Do not edit `run-r-job.sh` in place.** This folder is refreshed every time
> you open RStudio, so your changes would be gone the next time you log in —
> with no warning and no copy kept.

Two file names are replaced on every launch:

| File | |
|---|---|
| `run-r-job.sh` | the wrapper — replaced every launch |
| `README.md` | this file — replaced every launch |

**Everything else you put in this folder is left alone.** That includes
`run-my-job.sh`, your R scripts, and your output files.

Broke something? Open RStudio again. A correct copy comes back.

---

## Why a wrapper at all

Your RStudio session runs inside a container image — a fixed R and a fixed set
of packages, the same for everyone in the course. A batch job runs on a
different machine, which has an R of its own and none of the course packages.
Left alone, your job would use the wrong R and fail to find things that work
perfectly in the console.

The wrapper closes that gap. It runs your script:

- in the course image, `<%= _image %>`, so the R version is the one you have been using;
- with the course package library on `.libPaths()`, so `library(...)` works;
- with your own package library too, so anything you installed yourself still loads;
- with your home directory visible, so your files and paths are where you expect.

If you would rather run it by hand, this is what it comes down to:

```bash
. /shared/spack/share/spack/setup-env.sh
spack env activate apptainer
apptainer exec -B /shared/courseSharedFolders -B "$HOME" \
  --env R_LIBS_USER="$HOME/R/%p-library/%v:<%= _rlib %>" \
  <%= _image %> Rscript hw3.R
```

The wrapper does exactly that, and reads the image and library paths from the
course at run time — so a copy you made in September still uses the right ones
in November.

---

## If something goes wrong

| What you see | What it usually means |
|---|---|
| `sbatch: command not found` | You are in the wrong place — use the **Terminal** pane inside RStudio, not the Console. |
| No `.out` file at all | The job has not started yet. Check `squeue -u $USER`. |
| `there is no package called '...'` | The package is not in the course library. Install it in the RStudio console with `install.packages()` — it goes into your own library, which jobs can see. |
| Output stops in the middle | The job hit the time limit. Resubmit with a longer `-t`. |
| The job ignored your `-c` or `-t` | The option came after the script name. See above. |
| `run-r-job.sh: No such file` | Use the full path: `<%= _tools %>/run-r-job.sh`. |

Still stuck? Ask on the course forum, and include the job ID and the last few
lines of the `.out` file.
